{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## 740d triage reversed a recorded disposition -- it is a genuine defect\n\nThe ticket carried two prior RCA comments concluding 'host-environment artifact, no code fix\nwarranted'. That is wrong, and the project's own history settles it without needing a judgement call.\n\nRoot cause: git refuses to DISCOVER a bare repo via -C/cwd when safe.bareRepository=explicit; it\naccepts one named by top-level --git-dir. The _git helper in\ntests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_atomic_completion_close.py:40-46 hardcodes git -C <repo>, so the five\ntests reading the bare fixture remote die with exit 128 BEFORE any ref lookup.\n\nTwo claims in the original report are false and were sending readers down dead ends:\n - 'refs/heads/tickets does not exist' -- it exists and resolves fine; git never gets that far.\n - 'git 2.55.0' -- safe.bareRepository shipped in git 2.38.0 (2022-10-03) with exactly these\n   semantics. Four years stable. No version regression to chase.\n\nMeasured: 17 passed under default config isolated; 546 passed across the whole lifecycle directory;\n5 failed / 12 passed under safe.bareRepository=explicit. No pollution, no ordering dependency -- one\nconfig value governs the split entirely. Reproduced with an ordinary user gitconfig, no\nGIT_CONFIG_COUNT injection, so it is not a harness artifact.\n\nWHY IT IS A RECURRENCE, AND THE PART THAT MATTERS: closed ticket 3476-4472-3407-4246\n(embolismic-adverse-dragon) fixed this exact class across 13 files and added the held-out oracle\ntests/integration/test_bare_repository_fixture_portability_heldout.py. It also recorded the policy\nverbatim: 'the supported test suite must run both locally and in CI. Treat agents dismissing\nreproducible local test failures as environmental noise as an anti-pattern.' The guard missed this\nfile because it pins a HARDCODED 3-entry _FIXTURE_NODES allowlist, and\ntest_atomic_completion_close.py landed 2026-08-25 (commit 1cb1cdd25aff) -- four days after the guard\nand three days before 3476 closed. No static sweep exists anywhere (scripts/, Makefile,\n.pre-commit-config.yaml); scripts/check_raw_git_writes.py covers an unrelated concern.\n\nSo fixing only the five call sites would leave exactly the gap that produced this recurrence. AC4\ntherefore targets the allowlist itself: the guard's coverage must be derived from the tree, or an\nunregistered bare-fixture test must fail. This is the same lesson as the 2593 fix, where a\nhard-coded module list was replaced by a tree-derived scan -- twice in one day a hand-maintained\nallowlist silently stopped guarding.\n\nTicket retitled around safe.bareRepository=explicit, description rewritten with the measurements,\nfile_impact set to the two files, linked relates_to 3476, and the disposition correction recorded as\na comment. Fix subagent dispatched with both halves required and AC3's mutation proof mandatory.\n\nCROSS-CUTTING LESSON now in every subagent brief: putting the worktree venv first on PATH is not\nonly about the commit hook resolving ruff/mypy -- it also determines WHICH REBAR BUILD evaluates a\ngate. The global pipx rebar on this host is ~195 commits stale and its verdicts 'can be wrong in\nways the verdict cannot show'. Briefs now require confirming 'which rebar' resolves into the\nworktree and reading the gate's own build_drift line before trusting a verdict."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788481306312364001,"uuid":"b61bb540-875a-4b88-a769-cbbdea5f7083"}